<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Schweinfurth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Schweinfurth was a Baltic German botanist and explorer born in Riga. On his journey through the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> basin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> from 1868 to 1871 he reached the Uele river and became the first European to describe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a> and the Mangbetu, including the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munza">Munza</a>. His account, The Heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, was widely read.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-schweinfurth.jpg" alt="Georg Schweinfurth" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgschweinfurth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georg August Schweinfurth was born in Riga on 29 December 1836, when Riga was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. He studied at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> between 1856 and 1862, devoting himself especially to botany and palaeontology. He was asked to arrange the collections that Adalbert von Barnim and Robert Hartmann had brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, which turned his attention to the region. In 1863 he travelled round the shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, repeatedly crossed the district between that sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1868 the Alexander von Humboldt Foundation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> entrusted him with a scientific mission to the interior. Starting from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> in January 1869, he went up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> to Bahr-el-Ghazal and then travelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> dealers through the lands of the Diur, Dinka, Bongo and Niam-Niam. Crossing the Congo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> watershed he entered the country of the Mangbetu and reached the river Uele on 19 March 1870. Because it flowed west, he knew it was independent of the Nile and concluded that it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> system; it was several years before its link with the Congo was shown. A fire in his camp in December 1870 destroyed nearly all his collections. He returned to Khartoum in July 1871.</p><p>He described the Mangbetu in detail, including their cannibalistic practices, and his discovery of the pygmy Aka people settled the question of whether dwarf races existed in tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 1873-1874 he accompanied Friedrich Gerhard Rohlfs into the Libyan Desert. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1875 and founded a geographical society under the auspices of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a>. In 1876 he travelled into the Arabian Desert with Paul Güssfeldt, and until 1888 he continued exploring there and made geological and botanical studies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Fayum</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1889 and visited the Italian colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> in 1891, 1892 and 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His account of the expedition appeared as Im Herzen von Afrika (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, 1874); the English edition, The Heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, came out in 1873. He also published Artes Africanae in 1875 and Arabische Pflanzennamen aus Ägypten, Algerien und Jemen in 1912. The discovery of the Uele was his chief geographical achievement, but his additions to knowledge of the peoples, flora and fauna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> were considered of greater importance. The eastern chimpanzee Pan troglodytes schweinfurthii is named after him, and his botanical specimens are held at institutions including the Kew Herbarium. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 19 September 1925.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georg August Schweinfurth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The botanist <b>Georg Schweinfurth</b> described the court of King Munza in 1870 in vivid detail.</blockquote><a href="/state/mangbetu-kingdom">Mangbetu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1893" aria-label="Riga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Georg Schweinfurth"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4726 5249h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4726 5249h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1893">What was here in 1893? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
